cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this development, please cite it as below. The formalization
  paper describing it (in preparation) and per-result papers will supersede
  this repository-level entry as they are posted; arXiv IDs / DOIs will be
  added here when they exist.
title: >-
  lean-algorithmic-learning-theory: machine-checked algorithmic learning
  theory in Lean 4 (statistical queries, MDL discovery, Ville's inequality,
  a verified poly-step learner, realizability categories, bounded Gödel
  decisions)
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Palamarchuk
    given-names: Mykola
    email: lividgreen@gmail.com
    orcid: https://orcid.org/0009-0007-2106-5777
    affiliation: Independent researcher
repository-code: https://github.com/lividgreen/lean-algorithmic-learning-theory
url: https://lividgreen.github.io/lean-algorithmic-learning-theory/
abstract: >-
  A Lean 4 + Mathlib development machine-checking the query-model,
  information-theoretic and computational side of algorithmic learning
  theory: Ville's inequality for non-negative supermartingales; a
  statistical-query framework with the version-space envelope and parity's
  exponential SQ dimension; a native step-and-workspace cost model over
  Mathlib's partial recursive functions, carrying a decision-list learner
  verified both correct and polynomial-step; a realizability category over
  Kleene's first algebra with a capacity-bounded recursor; and a bounded
  Gödel decision over an existing formalization of the first incompleteness
  theorem. No sorry, no native_decide, standard axioms only, with every
  capstone's axiom footprint asserted by a build-enforced guard.
license: Apache-2.0
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21284775
# Deliberately only the concept DOI (decision 2026-07-28). Per-version DOIs are
# minted by Zenodo *after* a release exists, so a version row can only be added
# in a commit that comes after the tag — which means every tagged artifact would
# lack its own DOI and carry its predecessor's, permanently one behind. Zenodo's
# record page already lists every version and its DOI authoritatively, and the
# concept DOI below always resolves to the latest, so an in-repo list duplicates
# generated data and can only be stale. Cite the concept DOI for the work, or
# the version DOI of the version you used — that one is on the Zenodo record.
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.21284775
    description: >-
      Concept DOI — resolves to the latest archived version, and its Zenodo
      record lists the per-version DOIs.
keywords:
  - Lean 4
  - Mathlib
  - formal verification
  - algorithmic learning theory
  - statistical queries
  - minimum description length
  - Ville's inequality
  - realizability
  - Kolmogorov complexity
  - Gödel incompleteness
